<details class="gloss">
  <summary>Plain-language guide to the terms</summary>
  <dl>
    <dt>Trust</dt>
    <dd>How willing someone is to follow the advice. In these studies, people rated this from 0 (not at all) to 10 (completely).</dd>
    <dt>Quantitative reasoning</dt>
    <dd>Deciding with numbers, data and statistics, like reading a lab result.</dd>
    <dt>Intuition</dt>
    <dd>Deciding with experience and judgment, like a doctor sensing that something is off when the tests look fine.</dd>
    <dt>Effect size (Cohen's d)</dt>
    <dd>How big a difference is, on a scale that can be compared across studies. Around 0.2 is small, 0.5 is medium, and 0.8 or more is large.</dd>
    <dt>95% interval</dt>
    <dd>The range of values the true average probably falls in. A narrow range means a precise estimate; a range that includes zero means the difference may not be real.</dd>
    <dt>p value</dt>
    <dd>How surprising the result would be if there were no real difference. A p below .05 is the usual bar for calling a result reliable, and "p &lt; .001" means chance is a very unlikely explanation.</dd>
    <dt>Mixed model</dt>
    <dd>A way to analyze data when each person gives many ratings, so that people who rate everything high or low do not distort the results.</dd>
    <dt>Mediation</dt>
    <dd>A test of whether one thing explains part of the link between two others. Here: does seeing a doctor as more competent explain part of why people trust the doctor more?</dd>
    <dt>Preregistered</dt>
    <dd>The plan for the study and its analysis was written and posted publicly before anyone looked at the data.</dd>
  </dl>
</details>
